  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "K.Natarajan vs S.Sudarsanan on 7 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Thursday, The 7Th Day Of January 2016/17Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 2182 of 2007 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 432/2000 of Additional District & Sessions Court (Fast Track-1), Thiruvananthapuram Cc 8/1993 of Addl.C.J.M.,Thiruvananthapuram Revision Petitioner(S): ------------------------------------ K.Natarajan, Indira Bhavan, Venpalavattom, Anayara P.O. By Advs.Sri.Sasthamangalam S. Ajithkumar Sri.Prabhu Vijayakumar Respondent(S): ------------------------ S.Sudarsanan, Food Inspector, Thiruvananthapuram Circle, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala Ernakulam. By Public Prosecutor, Shri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 07-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.2182 of 2007 K.Natarajan vs S.Sudarsanan on 7 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/6412785/ 1 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 7th day of January 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 7.11.1992 at about 11.30 a.m., PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 750 grams of dhall channa from the shop of the revision petitioner. First accused was the salesman of the said shop. Third accused supplied the item to the second accused. Samples were taken in accordance with law and thereafter, one of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P13 report stating that the food item was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW5 were examined and Exts. P1 to P29 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 to DW3 were examined and Exts.D1 to D8(a) were marked for the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as provided under Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFAAct, Ext. P13 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P13 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as provided under Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFAAct, Ext. P13 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P13 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused."
  },
